               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="130X/131X" notBefore="1300" notAfter="1319" calendar="#gregorian">s. xiv in</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#i" source="893"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_94">Cambridge,
                        Cambridgeshire. Franciscan convent</orgName><p>'Iste liber constat fratri
                        Thome Trumpyton ordinis minorum sacre theologie professoris, precium viij
                        marc' (fol. 1v).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Has cauciones (Cambridge) of Hugh Damlett
                     (afterwards master of Pembroke and rector of St Peter-upon-Cornhill, London)
                     dated 1435 and 1438, and of John Clynt (cf. Peterhouse MSS 77, 255, 263 and
                     Gonville and Caius MS 510) dated 1441. Later belonged to Thomas Trumpyngton
                     STP, OFM (who in 1465 was chaplain of the house of Poor Clares at Denny, Cambs,
                     and owned BL MS Cotton Cleopatra C.ix fols. 63--165 and perhaps Vatican MS
                     Ottobon. lat. 352).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Given to Balliol by William Gray, bishop of
                     Ely (d. 1478).</provenance>
               <provenance type="#e" source="7768"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire.
                        Balliol College (Domus de Balliolo)</orgName><p>‘From some religious house
                        (possibly a friary in the Eastern counties), to judge by two almost
                        contemporary inscriptions: (a) 1v, perhaps partly cut off by the binder, pro
                        anima Alicie de Depedene | matris fratris Nicholai de eadem; (b) 2, Pro
                        anima . . . qui dedit totam summam Bonaventure in hoc volumine et al . . .
                        eadem auctoritate. . . . [. . . ] The volume was later in Cambridge, as is
                        shown by erased notes of pledging [many cited]. Another owner (the order in
                        time is not clear) was a Cambridge Franciscan. on 1v is: Iste liber constat
                        fratri Thome Trumpyton ordinis minorum sacre theologie professori: prec.
                        viij marc., and on 277v Caucio Trumpyngton fratris minoris . . . cum uno
                        supplemento . . . vez. pro xxvi s. cum viij d.’ (Mynors, Balliol, 113)‘Liber
                        domus de Balliolo in Oxon’ | ex dono Willelmi Gray Eliensis episcopi’ (fol.
                        3v)</p></provenance>
                  </history>
